#. You can block executable content in e-mail attachments\nby highlighting the executable attachment types you wish to\nblock. E-mail containing these attachment types will \nbe automatically returned to the sender.
msgid "mai_DESC_BLOCK_EXECUTABLE_CONTENT"
msgstr ""

#. Your server includes a complete, full-featured e-mail server. However,\nif for some reason you wish to delegate e-mail processing to\nanother system, specify the IP address of the delegate system\nhere. For normal operation, leave this field blank.
msgid "mai_DESC_DELEGATE"
msgstr ""

#. For ETRN or multi-drop, you can control how frequently this server\ncontacts your secondary e-mail server to fetch e-mail. More\nfrequent connections mean that you receive your e-mail more\nquickly, but also cause Internet requests to be sent more often,\npossibly increasing your phone and Internet charges.
msgid "mai_DESC_FETCH_PERIOD"
msgstr ""

#. You can control IMAP server access. The setting 'Allow access\nonly from local networks' allows IMAP access only from your\nlocal network(s). The IMAPS setting can be used to provide\nencrypted external access to your IMAP server. We recommend\nleaving this setting 'Allow access only from local networks'\nunless you have a specific reason to do otherwise.
msgid "mai_DESC_IMAP_ACCESS_CONTROL"
msgstr ""

#. The e-mail retrieval mode can be set to\nstandard (for dedicated Internet connections), ETRN (recommended\nfor dialup connections), or multi-drop (for dialup connections if\nETRN is not supported by your Internet provider). Note that\nmulti-drop mode is the only option available when the server is\nconfigured in private server and gateway mode.
msgid "mai_DESC_MODE"
msgstr ""

#. Usually 25, 587 or 465. If empty will assume 25.
msgid "mai_DESC_PEERPORT"
msgstr ""

#. You can control POP3 server access. The setting 'Allow access\nonly from local networks' allows POP3 access only from your\nlocal network(s). The POP3S setting can be used to provide\nencrypted external access to your POP3 server. We recommend\nleaving this setting 'Allow access only from local networks'\nunless you have a specific reason to do otherwise.
msgid "mai_DESC_POP_ACCESS_CONTROL"
msgstr ""

#. For ETRN or multi-drop, specify the hostname or IP address of your\nsecondary mail server. (If using the standard e-mail setup, this\nfield can be left blank.)
msgid "mai_DESC_SECONDARY"
msgstr ""

#. The server can deliver outgoing messages directly to their\ndestination (recommended in most cases) or can deliver them via\nyour Internet provider's SMTP server (recommended if you have an\nunreliable Internet connection or are using a residential Internet\nservice). If using your Internet provider's SMTP server, specify\nits hostname or IP address below. Otherwise leave this field blank.
msgid "mai_DESC_SMARTHOST"
msgstr ""

#. You can provide authenticated access to your SMTP server, or \nset it to Disabled.\nThe SSMTP setting requires <b>all</b> users to use SSL/TLS \nauthentication. The SMTP and SSMTP option additionally allows \nSTARTTLS to be used to ensure secure authentication.
msgid "mai_DESC_SMTP_AUTH_CONTROL"
msgstr ""

#. You can scan e-mail for spam. If Spam filtering is\nenabled, an X-Spam-Status: header is added to each\nmessage, which can be used for filtering spam.\nYou can adjust the sensitivity of the Spam detection\nprocess from the default of medium. For fine-grained\ncontrol, you can set the Spam sensitivity to Custom\nand then choose a custom tagging level, and \noptionally a level at which to reject the message.
msgid "mai_DESC_SPAM_SCAN"
msgstr ""

#. You can enable to add a tag to the subject of each\nmessage that is classified as SPAM.\nThe value for this tag can be defined below.
msgid "mai_DESC_SPAM_SUBJECT"
msgstr ""

